# The intro sequence (world expansion 1, plan section 0). Every card is `class: story`, tagged `intro`: left is always
# the quick way (a line, nothing else); right engages, with at most a line, a fact or a tiny warm-up. Never a cost.
#   The orientation (feedback 2026-10-20): one condensed run of cards at the very start, before the registrar's list and
#   enrolment. The Welcome asks first: left skips it, right plays along, and then each card leads straight to the next
#   whichever side you take: Marrow's question (what is just? asked again on the eve of graduation, isolde_asks_again),
#   the tour (where you meet Bram; following him shows you the places that matter, intro_places), the wrong tower (a
#   negotiation lesson), lunch, Vey's tea (a check lesson), your room, the safety talk (slice_ward_lesson, so
#   you know Ward before the practice fight), the practice circle (a fight lesson, coached exchange by exchange) and your
#   kit. Skipping never skips Ward: the safety talk then comes in its usual place, after enrolment.
#   The lessons' checks are fair (even odds, pitched at a first-year's skill: feedback 2026-10-20), and their only
#   consequence is a tiny change of Mood.
#   Just-in-time cards: once each, the first time a system matters; off with the title screen's "Skip the introductions"
#   or the Settings row (flag skip_intros).
#   The kit card (intro_kit) is Thorne's, not Mir's (the plan said Mir): it would pre-empt Mir's own first meeting.

# ---------------- The orientation ----------------

@event intro_welcome
title: The Welcome
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: auto
when: turn == 1
priority: 90
slot: morning
art: crown
tags: intro story
scene: The Great Hall full of new first-years under floating candles, a tall headmistress at the lectern.
text: Floating candles, two hundred first-years trying not to look up. Headmistress Marrow speaks for four minutes: four years of twelve moons, a name that travels, and a castle that remembers who you were in it. "Before you choose your courses," she ends, "the prefects will show you how things work here. Those who think they know already may go straight to the registrar."
> left: Skip the orientation
  say: You slip out ahead of the crowd to find the registrar's list. Whatever the castle has to teach you, you'll learn it the hard way. (The safety talk is compulsory: you'll still have that.)
> right: Stay for the orientation
  queue intro_what_is_just now
  say: You stay. The hall empties toward the prefects, and the headmistress comes down from the lectern, straight toward you.

# Marrow's question (feedback 2026-10-20): what you answer is remembered, and nothing else. On the eve of graduation she
# asks again (isolde_asks_again, calendar.grim) and says whether your four years kept to it.
@event intro_what_is_just
title: What Is Just?
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: queued
type: menu
art: scales
tags: intro story
scene: A tall headmistress with a small notebook stopping in front of one first-year in an emptying hall, candles guttering overhead.
text: Headmistress Marrow stops in front of you, notebook open, as if she'd been looking for you in particular. "One question before the prefects have you," she says. "I ask it of everyone. Very few remember. What do you think is just?"
> mercy: "Mercy."
  hint: Giving people more than they've earned.
  flag just_mercy
  learn isolde.mercy
  queue intro_tour now
  say: "Mercy," she repeats, and writes it down. "Expensive. Most people who say it haven't been asked to pay for it yet." She closes the notebook on her finger. "Ask me again in four years."
> fair: "Fairness."
  hint: One rule, the same for everyone.
  flag just_fair
  learn isolde.mercy
  queue intro_tour now
  say: "Fairness," she repeats, and writes it down. "The same rule for the duke and the pickpocket. Harder than it sounds, when you know one of them." She closes the notebook on her finger. "Ask me again in four years."
> strong: "Keeping your own safe."
  hint: Whatever it takes, for the people who are yours.
  flag just_strong
  learn isolde.mercy
  queue intro_tour now
  say: "Keeping your own safe," she repeats, and writes it down. "An honest answer. Most people dress it up." She closes the notebook on her finger. "Ask me again in four years. I'd like to know who you decided was yours."

@event intro_tour
title: The Prefect's Tour
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
art: map
tags: intro story
actor: bram
on_show:
  meet bram
scene: A prefect in a red sash leading a straggle of first-years through a castle corridor, holding up a ledger like a flag.
text: A burly prefect in a red sash gathers the first-years with a ledger. "Bram Hollis. Prefect. Tour. Each day you get a few free hours, and you spend them on the map: one place an hour. Some doors open only once you've earned them. Some never open for you. Keep up."
> left: Skip the tour
  queue intro_wrong_tower now
  say: You slip away and find your own way round. You get lost twice and find the kitchens once, which is a fair trade.
> right: Follow him round
  queue intro_places now
  say: Bram walks you past every door and names its rules. At a blank stretch of wall, a boy with a lockpick behind his ear winks at you, as if the wall were a joke you'd both get later.

# The tour's second half (feedback 2026-10-20): the places with the most uses, named once, by the prefect who'd rather
# not. Every one is open from the first day.
@event intro_places
title: Where Things Are
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
actor: bram
art: map
tags: intro story
scene: A prefect reading from a ledger at the top of a castle stair, pointing down corridors with his quill, first-years craning to follow.
text: Bram reads the castle to you off his ledger. "Job Board, by the stairs: work and wages. Library: revision tables before every exam. Infirmary, on the Alchemy floor: Sister Amaranth, for when you break something, yourself included. The village, down the hill: robes and wands on market day, tea at Mabel's, tonics at Pell's." He snaps the ledger shut. "Questions? No. Good."
> left: "No questions."
  queue intro_wrong_tower now
  say: "Excellent," says Bram, and means it. He leaves you at the foot of the stairs with a map in your head and a strong sense of having been processed.
> right: Ask what he left out
  queue intro_wrong_tower now
  say: He looks pained. "The Gilded Newt, in the village, after dark: supper, cider and people you shouldn't be seen with. The Lakeshore, for thinking. That's all." He writes something in the ledger. It might be your name.

@event intro_wrong_tower
title: The Wrong Tower
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
art: scales
tags: intro story
scene: A harassed house steward behind a desk heaped with lists, a student's trunk visible through a doorway in the wrong tower.
text: Your trunk has gone to the wrong tower. The house steward has a list, and the list says it hasn't. "If it's on the list," she says, "it's where it should be." Behind her, through a door, you can see your trunk with your name on it.
> left: Carry it over yourself
  queue intro_lunch now
  queue intro_lunch_alone now
  say: You haul it across two bridges and up ninety stairs. The steward ticks something on the list as you pass, satisfied that it was always right.
> right: Talk her round
  queue intro_lunch now
  queue intro_lunch_alone now
  challenge negotiate novice+:
    win:
      mood +tiny
      say: You ask to see the list. Then the line. Then the tower on the line. The steward goes pink, corrects it in red ink and sends two porters. "Clerical," she says.
    partial:
      say: She won't change the list, but she lends you a porter "as a courtesy". The porter and the list never speak again.
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      say: She has an answer for everything, most of it the list. You carry the trunk yourself in the end, but you've learned how she argues, which is worth a tower.

@event intro_lunch
title: The Lunch Table
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: queued
art: meal
tags: intro story
cast:
  friend: any where met(friend) and tagged(friend, peer) and not npc_is(bram)
scene: Long refectory tables at noon, first-years spread along the benches, one empty place beside a familiar face.
text: Lunch in the Great Hall, under the gallery where the refectory tables run. You can sit anywhere. At noon people find you, or you find them; either way, who you sit with tends to become who you know.
> left: Eat with whoever's there
  queue intro_vey_tea now
  say: You squeeze in between two strangers who are arguing about soup. By pudding they're not strangers, quite.
> right: Sit with {friend}
  queue intro_vey_tea now
  rel friend +tiny
  say: {friend} shifts along the bench to make room. You talk about nothing much, which is how most friendships start.

# Lunch for a player who has met no fellow student yet (usual at this point): the card above needs someone to sit with,
# and every peer has a first meeting of their own that lunch mustn't take. Both are queued; exactly one plays. Bram, met
# on the tour, is a prefect, not a lunch companion: he counts for neither.
@event intro_lunch_alone
title: The Lunch Table
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: queued
when: not met(lyra) and not met(tobias) and not met(seraphine) and not met(corvin) and not met(mir) and not met(kael) and not met(nessa) and not met(ilse)
art: meal
tags: intro story
scene: Long refectory tables at noon, first-years spread along the benches, a student with a tray looking for a place.
text: Lunch in the Great Hall, under the gallery where the refectory tables run. You can sit anywhere, and you don't know anyone yet. At noon people find you, or you find them; either way, who you sit with tends to become who you know.
> left: Eat with whoever's there
  queue intro_vey_tea now
  say: You squeeze in between two strangers who are arguing about soup. By pudding they're not strangers, quite.
> right: Sit at the end and listen
  queue intro_vey_tea now
  say: You take the end of a bench and listen. By the end of the meal you know who runs the Ledger, who duels, who talks to plants, and that the girl with the floating quill will find you whether you like it or not.

@event intro_vey_tea
title: Tea That Isn't There
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
art: cup
tags: intro story
scene: A corridor alcove, a sharp-eyed professor holding out an empty hand as if it held a teacup, a student hesitating.
text: Professor Vey stops you in a corridor and offers you a cup of tea. There is no cup. There is no tea. She waits to see what you'll do. "Every attempt has its odds," she says pleasantly. "Skill moves them. Luck moves them less. Drink up."
> left: Decline the tea
  queue intro_room now
  say: "No, thank you," you say. Vey sighs, drinks the tea that isn't there, and walks on. You feel as though you've failed something small.
> right: Pretend to drink it
  queue intro_room now
  challenge deceive novice-:
    win:
      learn vey.tests
      mood +tiny
      say: You sip, wince at the heat and compliment the blend. Vey laughs, delighted, and writes something down. "Points," she says. "Nobody compliments the blend."
    partial:
      learn vey.tests
      say: You drink it convincingly and then try to hand back the cup. There is no cup. Vey smiles. "Half points," she says.
    lose:
      learn vey.tests
      mood -tiny
      say: You laugh halfway through the sip. Vey sighs, theatrically. "Most do," she says. "I keep score, you know. Everyone starts at nought."

@event intro_room
title: Your Room
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: queued
art: bed
tags: intro story
scene: A narrow tower room with a bed, a trunk at its foot and a window full of lake, a folded note on the pillow.
text: Your room in the tower: a bed, a trunk, a window with half the lake in it. On the pillow, a note from the bursar, folded twice.
> left: Unpack later
  queue slice_ward_lesson now
  queue intro_practice_circle now
  say: You fall onto the bed in your robes and sleep like a dropped stone. The note is still under your cheek in the morning.
> right: Read the bursar's note
  queue slice_ward_lesson now
  queue intro_practice_circle now
  say: Board is paid each month, it says, or you go hungry. Sleep mends the body and the mood; a bad night shows. Mana comes back on its own. And please do not feed the gargoyles.

@event intro_practice_circle
title: The Practice Circle
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: queued
art: duel
tags: intro story
scene: A chalk circle on a scorched duelling-hall floor, a straw mannequin standing in it, a grizzled professor watching from the edge.
text: Professor Thorne has chalked a circle and stood a straw mannequin in it. "Every first-year fights it once," he says. "It moves first, and it shows you what it means to do. Watch it. Lose to it if you must. Losing well is the only thing worth teaching."
> left: Watch from the bench
  queue intro_kit now
  say: You watch three first-years go down to the mannequin, then one beat it. Thorne says the same thing to all four: "Good. Again."
> right: Step into the circle
  queue intro_kit now
  challenge combat novice certain:
    win:
      mood +tiny
      say: The mannequin topples in a puff of straw. Thorne nods. "Now lose one sometime," he says. "Then you'll know what you're doing."
    partial:
      say: You trade blows until the bell. Thorne looks pleased. "A draw is a fight you both learned from," he says.
    lose:
      learn thorne.lost
      mood -tiny
      say: The mannequin wins, which is humiliating and educational. Thorne helps you up. "I lost to a storm once," he says. "Badly. Losing well is the whole art."

@event intro_kit
title: What You Carry
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
art: wand
tags: intro story
scene: A grizzled professor tapping a student's battered borrowed wand, a robe and a small pouch laid out on a bench.
text: Afterwards Thorne taps your borrowed wand with one finger. "Held together with hope," he says. "What you carry matters in a fight, and only there: a wand, a robe, one thing in your pocket. What you own opens other doors. Keep your kit in order."
> left: Later
  say: You nod and put the wand away. It sparks once, sideways, as if it had heard.
> right: Show me
  say: He shows you: what you wear only counts when you fight; what you own lets you do things on the right day, like a spell does. Your kit is on its own tab, beside the others. "And trade that wand in," he says, "when you can."

# ---------------- Just in time (off with skip_intros) ----------------

@pool intro_auto_story
trigger: auto
class: story
priority: 30
tags: intro

@event jit_after_curfew
title: After Curfew
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: eerie
when: turn == 3 and not flag(skip_intros)
slot: night
art: lantern
scene: A dark castle corridor after curfew, a prefect's lantern far off at one end, a student pressed into a doorway.
text: After curfew the castle changes hands. The corridors belong to prefects and to whatever else walks them. You can stay in and sleep, or slip out at midnight to wherever is open then. Get caught often enough and people start to notice.
> left: Stay in tonight
  say: You listen to the castle settle and decide midnight can wait.
> right: Watch the prefect's lantern go by
  say: The lantern passes your door, pauses and moves on. You learn the round by heart. It feels like knowing a secret.

@event jit_red_string
title: Red String
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: eerie
when: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and not flag(skip_intros)
art: paper
scene: A student's desk by candlelight, scraps of paper pinned to the wall above it, joined by lengths of red string.
text: You've started keeping what you notice: a scrap of paper here, a remembered word there. Tonight you pin them to the wall above your desk and join them with red string. It doesn't make sense yet. It isn't supposed to.
> left: Go to bed
  say: You leave the string where it is. In the morning it looks like a constellation you don't know the name of.
> right: Stare at it a while
  say: The things you've found are all there in your notes now, and the leads you haven't followed. Nobody will make you follow them. That's the frightening part.

@event jit_exam_nerves
title: Exam Nerves
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: auto
when: month == 4 and term and year == 1 and not flag(skip_intros)
priority: 95
slot: morning
art: hourglass
tags: intro
scene: A queue of pale first-years outside a closed examination hall door, one of them reciting under their breath.
text: Exams. Outside the hall someone is reciting the stirring count under their breath. Every class you went to and every night you revised is in your head somewhere, and how much of it is there is most of what decides the paper.
> left: Walk straight in
  say: You walk in before the nerves can catch you. The door shuts like a held breath.
> right: Go over your notes once more
  say: Preparation counts more than talent here, a fourth-year once told you. You hope she was right. She usually is.

@event jit_long_break
title: The Long Break
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: auto
when: break and year == 1 and not flag(skip_intros)
priority: 90
slot: morning
art: house
tags: intro
scene: An emptying castle courtyard, trunks being loaded onto a barge, a few students waving and a few staying behind.
text: The term is over and the castle empties. {off_campus: You watch it go from the barge rail, your trunk at your feet.|} A break is your own: no lectures, and four free visits a day if you stay at the castle or go home, three if you take on paid work. Some friends leave; some stay. Letters come instead.
> left: Make plans later
  say: {off_campus: You put your feet up for an afternoon first. Home is very quiet after the castle, and nobody rings a bell at you.|You put your feet up for an afternoon first. The castle is very quiet, and very much yours.}
> right: {off_campus: Walk round home|Walk the empty halls}
  say: {off_campus: Everything is smaller than you remember. Your old room is full of apples now, and someone has kept your bed made anyway.|Your footsteps echo. The kitchens are open all day on a break, the cook says, and the cook is lonely.}

@event jit_counter
title: Across the Counter
place: village
class: story
tone: light
when: not flag(skip_intros)
weight: 40
art: coin
tags: intro shop
scene: A village shop counter seen from a student's side, a price slate, a few goods and a shopkeeper waiting patiently.
text: The village sells what the castle doesn't. Every shop has its counter, with its prices on the slate; some things are only offered once you've proved something, or know someone, or can pay a great deal. And the pawnbroker buys back what you're tired of.
> left: Browse another day
  say: You walk the square with your hands in your pockets. Everything in the windows looks expensive and possible.
> right: Read every slate
  say: Buns, robes, wands, tea, tonics, one at a time. You make a list of what you'd buy if you had money. It's a long list.

@event jit_real_fight
title: A Real Fight
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: tense
when: turn >= 6 and seen(intro_practice_circle) > 0 and not flag(skip_intros)
slot: evening
art: sword
scene: A duelling hall with a real duel under way, two students circling, a third nursing a split lip on the bench.
text: The mannequin was a lesson. Real fights aren't. Whoever you face fights to win, the way you would; Health lost below the line stays lost, and anything you'd rather not be seen casting, someone may see. You can always refuse a fight, at a price of its own.
> left: Nod and move on
  say: You file it away. Somewhere in the hall, someone is losing well. Thorne would be pleased.
> right: Watch a bout to the end
  say: You watch two third-years fight to a standstill, bow and go to supper together. It looked like the opposite of a lesson. It was the same thing.

@event jit_real_negotiation
title: A Real Negotiation
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: light
when: turn == 3 and seen(intro_wrong_tower) > 0 and not flag(skip_intros)
slot: afternoon
art: scales
scene: A student and an official facing each other across a desk heaped with papers, both leaning forward.
text: Not every talk is a quick word. Some are negotiations: someone across the table with reasons of their own, and something at stake. Charm, lore and guile all count, and so does how you come across today. The steward and her list were practice.
> left: Fair enough
  say: You resolve to argue only with people who aren't holding a ledger.
> right: Remember the steward and her list
  say: However it went with the steward, the lesson was the same: the list was her whole argument. You make a note: always ask to see the line.

@event jit_infirmary
title: A Word From the Infirmary
class: story
tone: warm
trigger: auto
when: health <= 30 and not flag(skip_intros)
priority: 70
art: potion
tags: intro
scene: A stern healer in a white apron writing on a chart at the foot of a student's bed.
text: Sister Amaranth catches you on the stairs, looks at you once and steers you to a bed. "Low Health," she says. "Under thirty, the body starts to lose things it doesn't get back easily. Sleep. Eat. Stop fighting things." She doesn't believe you'll listen.
> left: Promise to rest
  say: You promise. She writes it on your chart, which feels binding in a way promises usually don't.
> right: Ask how low is too low
  say: "Lower than you are," she says, "is too low." She gives you a tonic that tastes of iron and disapproval.

@event jit_something_more
title: Something More
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: warm
when: not flag(skip_intros)
art: heart
tags: romance
cast:
  x: any where romanceable(x) and spark(x) >= 9
scene: Two students in a crowded corridor, one of them looking back over their shoulder a moment longer than they meant to.
text: You catch yourself looking for {x} in every room. That's new. Friendship grows on its own; this is something else, and it grows only when you let it. It can also go cold if you leave it too long.
> left: Think about something else
  say: You think about something else, with great determination, for almost an hour.
> right: Admit it, to yourself at least
  say: You admit it to your pillow, which keeps it. Tomorrow you'll be braver. Possibly.

@event jit_rivals
title: Rivals
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: tense
when: bonds(rival) >= 1 and not flag(skip_intros)
art: duel
scene: Two students passing on a staircase, neither giving way, both pretending not to notice the other.
text: Somebody here has decided you're the one to beat. A rival watches what you do and does it better, or tries to, and you'll find yourself doing the same. Rivals can soften into something else. They can also sharpen.
> left: Ignore it
  say: You resolve to ignore it. You fail almost at once, which is how rivalry works.
> right: Get better
  say: You stay late in the library out of pure spite. It is extraordinarily motivating.

@event jit_mirror
title: The Mirror Remembers
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: eerie
when: (alignment >= 15 or alignment <= -15) and not flag(skip_intros)
art: mirror
scene: A student passing the tall sorting mirror in the entrance hall, their reflection a half-step behind them.
text: You pass the Sorting Mirror on the way to breakfast and your reflection is a half-step slow. {alignment >= 15: It looks brighter than you feel.|It looks colder than you feel.} What you do when it costs you something is being remembered, somewhere, and it leans you one way or the other.
> left: Walk faster
  say: Your reflection catches up by the end of the hall. You decide not to wonder how.
> right: Look back at it
  say: It looks back. Then it straightens its robes, which you hadn't, and you straighten yours.

@event jit_noticed
title: Noticed
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: light
when: renown >= 30 and not flag(skip_intros)
art: crown
scene: A student walking into a crowded common room as a few heads turn, someone whispering behind a hand.
text: Heads turn when you come into the common room. A first-year whispers your name to another. Being known opens doors and invitations; it also fades if you stop giving people something to talk about.
> left: Pretend not to notice
  say: You pretend not to notice. Everyone notices you pretending.
> right: Wave
  say: You wave. Three people wave back, and one of them has no idea who you are.

@event jit_first_shift
title: First Shift
pool: intro_auto_story
tone: light
when: member(job) and not flag(skip_intros)
art: coin
tags: job
scene: A student tying on an apron in a back room, a list of duties pinned on the wall beside them.
text: A job is a few shifts a term, shown on the map when they're due; each one pays, and a missed one costs the boss's good opinion. Work well and you're promoted. Turn up on a break and it's just a visit.
> left: Tie the apron and go
  say: You tie the apron backwards. Nobody tells you for an hour.
> right: Read the duty list twice
  say: You read it twice. The second time, you notice the line at the bottom: "Ask if unsure." You decide to be sure instead.

@event jit_applications
title: Applications
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: auto
when: month == 7 and year == 2 and not flag(skip_intros)
priority: 52
art: letter
tags: intro
scene: A noticeboard plastered with internship notices, a student copying deadlines into a notebook.
text: The internship notices are up. A summer's placement means a summer away doing the work, and a letter from a supervisor that follows you after. Each wants a course passed, or more, and asks once a year.
> left: Look later
  say: You walk past the board. The notices will still be there tomorrow, mostly.
> right: Copy the deadlines down
  say: You copy every deadline into your notebook in your best hand, and underline the ones you could actually meet.
